{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Higher body mass index (BMI) at diagnosis is associated with poorer overall survival in melanoma patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To test whether patients with elevated BMI at diagnosis have reduced survival time compared to those with normal or low BMI.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv.  2. Filter to patients with non‐missing height_cm and weight_kg.  3. Compute BMI = weight_kg / (height_cm/100)^2.  4. Categorize patients into underweight (BMI<18.5), normal (18.5≤BMI<25), overweight (25≤BMI<30), and obese (BMI≥30).  5. Merge BMI categories with survival data (vital_status, days_to_death, days_to_last_followup).  6. Perform Kaplan–Meier survival analysis across the four BMI groups and log‐rank test for overall difference.  7. Fit a Cox proportional hazards model with BMI as a continuous predictor and with BMI category as a factor, adjusting for age_at_diagnosis and gender.  8. Visualize survival curves by BMI category.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of patient_id, height_cm, weight_kg, BMI, BMI category, survival time, event status.  • Kaplan–Meier survival plots by BMI category with log‐rank p-value.  • Cox model summary: hazard ratios for BMI (continuous) and for BMI categories (factors), 95% CIs, p-values."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Male melanoma patients have a higher tumor mutational burden (TMB) than female patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To compare TMB distributions between male and female patients.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. Compute per‐patient TMB as total somatic mutations divided by callable megabases (or raw count if callable region is constant).  3. Merge TMB with patient gender.  4. Filter to patients with non‐missing gender and TMB.  5. Compare TMB between males and females using a two‐sided Wilcoxon rank‐sum test.  6. Fit a linear regression model predicting TMB from gender, adjusting for age_at_diagnosis.  7. Plot boxplots or violin plots of TMB by gender.",  
      "deliverables":"• Boxplot/violin plot of TMB by gender.  • Wilcoxon test statistic and p-value.  • Regression coefficients, R², and p-values for gender effect on TMB."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients harboring the BRAF V600E mutation exhibit shorter overall survival compared to BRAF wild‐type patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To evaluate the impact of BRAF V600E status on patient survival.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. Filter the mutation data for Hugo_Symbol='BRAF' and protein change HGVSp_Short containing 'V600E'.  3. For each patient, assign BRAF status = 'V600E' if any V600E mutation exists, else 'WildType'.  4. Merge BRAF status with clinical survival data (vital_status, days_to_death, days_to_last_followup, age_at_diagnosis, gender).  5. Perform Kaplan–Meier analysis comparing survival curves for V600E vs WildType; compute log‐rank test.  6. Fit a Cox proportional hazards model with BRAF status (binary) adjusting for age and gender.  7. Plot Kaplan–Meier survival curves for the two groups.",  
      "deliverables":"• Survival curves for BRAF V600E vs wild‐type with log‐rank p-value.  • Cox model summary: hazard ratio for V600E vs wild‐type, 95% CI, p-value.  • Table of patient_id, BRAF status, survival time, and event status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Melanoma tumors with ulceration exhibit higher tumor mutational burden than non‐ulcerated tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To compare TMB between patients with ulcerated and non‐ulcerated primary tumors.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. Compute per‐patient TMB.  3. From clinical data, extract ulceration_indicator ('YES' vs 'NO'); exclude missing.  4. Merge TMB with ulceration status.  5. Compare TMB distributions using Wilcoxon rank‐sum test between ulcerated and non‐ulcerated groups.  6. Fit a linear regression model predicting TMB from ulceration indicator, adjusting for age_at_diagnosis and gender.  7. Visualize with boxplots of TMB by ulceration status.",  
      "deliverables":"• Boxplot of TMB by ulceration status.  • Wilcoxon test statistic and p-value.  • Regression summary: coefficient for ulceration, R², p-value."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"CTLA4 gene expression increases with patient age at diagnosis in melanoma.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To assess the relationship between CTLA4 expression and patient age.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Identify the Ensembl ID for CTLA4 (e.g., ENSG00000163599, handling version suffix).  3. Extract CTLA4 expression for each sample and aggregate to patient level by mean.  4. Merge CTLA4 expression with age_at_diagnosis and gender.  5. Compute Spearman correlation between age_at_diagnosis and CTLA4 expression; test significance.  6. Fit a linear regression model: CTLA4_expr ~ age_at_diagnosis + gender.  7. Plot scatter plot of CTLA4 expression vs age with regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"• Spearman correlation coefficient and p-value.  • Regression coefficients, R², and p-values.  • Scatter plot with regression line of CTLA4 expression vs age."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Melanoma patients with TP53 mutations have higher expression of PDCD1 (PD‐1) compared to TP53 wild‐type patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To compare PDCD1 expression between TP53‐mutated and wild‐type tumors.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Determine TP53 mutation status per patient by filtering Hugo_Symbol='TP53'; status = 'Mut' if any mutation, else 'WT'.  3. Identify Ensembl ID for PDCD1 (e.g., ENSG00000188389).  4. Extract PDCD1 expression per sample, aggregate to patient mean.  5. Merge PDCD1 expression with TP53 status.  6. Compare expression between groups using Wilcoxon rank‐sum test.  7. Fit a linear model PDCD1_expr ~ TP53_status + age_at_diagnosis + gender.  8. Plot violin/boxplots of PDCD1 expression by TP53 status.",  
      "deliverables":"• Boxplot/violin plot of PDCD1 expression by TP53 mutation status.  • Wilcoxon test statistic and p-value.  • Linear model summary: coefficient for TP53 status, R², p-value."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Prior systemic therapy is associated with improved overall survival in melanoma patients.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To test whether patients who received any prior systemic therapy have longer survival than those who did not.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv.  2. Extract prior_systemic_therapy indicator ('YES' vs 'NO'); exclude missing.  3. Merge with survival data (vital_status, days_to_death, days_to_last_followup, age_at_diagnosis, gender).  4. Perform Kaplan–Meier analysis comparing 'YES' vs 'NO'; calculate log‐rank test.  5. Fit Cox proportional hazards model: survival ~ prior_systemic_therapy + age_at_diagnosis + gender.  6. Plot Kaplan–Meier curves for the two groups.",  
      "deliverables":"• Kaplan–Meier survival curves for prior systemic therapy vs none with log‐rank p-value.  • Cox model summary: hazard ratio for therapy, 95% CI, p-value.  • Table of patient_id, therapy status, survival time, event status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tumor mutational burden differs by submitted tumor location (primary vs regional vs distant sites).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"To compare TMB across categories of tumor specimen location.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. Compute per‐patient TMB.  3. Extract submitted_tumor_location and categorize into: Primary Tumor, Regional Lymph Node, Regional Cutaneous/Subcutaneous, Distant Metastasis.  4. Merge TMB with location category; exclude missing.  5. Perform a Kruskal–Wallis test to compare TMB across the four groups.  6. Conduct post‐hoc Dunn’s pairwise tests with Bonferroni correction.  7. Visualize with boxplots of TMB by location category.",  
      "deliverables":"• Boxplot of TMB by submitted tumor location.  • Kruskal–Wallis H statistic and p-value.  • Post‐hoc Dunn’s test table with adjusted p-values.  • Table of patient_id, tumor location category, and TMB."  
    }  
  }  
]}
